# Vite Static Assets: `public/` vs `src/assets/`

## Default: use `src/assets/`

Import assets into your source code. Vite hashes filenames in production builds (e.g., `logo.2d8efhg.png`), enabling long-term caching and automatic cache-busting. Small files may be inlined as base64.

```tsx
// CORRECT — imported asset, hashed in production
import logo from '@/assets/logo-emantto.png';

export const Logo = () => <img src={logo} alt="Logo" />;
```

Use `src/assets/` when:

- The file is referenced in `.tsx`, `.ts`, or `.css` source code (images, SVGs, fonts used via `@font-face` in CSS modules, etc.)
- You want build-time optimization (hashing, inlining, tree-shaking of unused assets)
- The filename can change between deploys (cache-busting)

## Exception: use `public/`

Files in `public/` are copied as-is to the build output root, with **no hashing, no processing, no inlining**. Reference them with absolute paths from `/`.

```tsx
// CORRECT — absolute path, no import needed
<link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
<img src="/logo512.png" />
```

Use `public/` **only** when:

- The file must keep its exact name and path (e.g., `favicon.ico`, `robots.txt`, `manifest.json`, `sitemap.xml`)
- The file is never imported in source code (e.g., referenced only in `index.html` or by external systems)
- The file is runtime-generated or runtime-injected (e.g., `config.js` / `config.js.template` processed by `entrypoint.sh` at container startup)
- The file must be served at a well-known URL (e.g., `/.well-known/*`, PWA icons referenced by `manifest.json`)
- Custom fonts loaded via `<link>` in `index.html` rather than CSS `@font-face`

## What NOT to put in `public/`

- Images, icons, or SVGs used in components — import them from `src/assets/` instead
- CSS files — use CSS modules or import them in your source
- JS/TS files — they bypass the module graph and won't be type-checked or tree-shaken
- Anything that benefits from hashing/cache-busting

## Current project layout

```
apps/web/
  public/
    config.js              # Runtime config (local dev values) — never imported, loaded via <script> in index.html
    config.js.template     # envsubst template for container startup — processed by entrypoint.sh
    favicon.ico            # Must keep exact name
    fonts/                 # Loaded via <link> in index.html
    index.html             # App shell
    logo192.png            # PWA icon referenced by manifest.json
    logo512.png            # PWA icon referenced by manifest.json
    manifest.json          # PWA manifest — must keep exact name/path
  src/assets/
    logo-emantto.png       # App logo imported in components
```

## Dynamic asset URLs

When the asset path depends on a variable, use the `new URL()` pattern instead of dynamic `import()`:

```ts
// CORRECT — Vite can analyze the static portion of the template literal
function getIcon(name: string): string {
  return new URL(`../assets/icons/${name}.svg`, import.meta.url).href;
}
```

The string template must contain a static path prefix so Vite can resolve it at build time. Fully dynamic strings (e.g., `new URL(variable, import.meta.url)`) are left as-is and will break in production.

## CSS asset references

CSS `url()` references are processed by Vite the same way as JS imports — use relative paths:

```css
/* CORRECT — relative path, Vite resolves and hashes */
.hero {
  background-image: url('../assets/hero-bg.jpg');
}
```
